[{:seon.task/name "seon-source-is-the-program"
  :seon.task/plan "Session LOOP. A typed load effect reads next/src/** with Jolt's reader (exact text, positions, file order) and the analyzer (analysis only), so seon.* and my.* namespaces have form/var/ref facts on main and agents can change platform functions on their branches like any program code. The compiled binary is the running generation; main's facts are the next generation's source.

Where this branch stands (session LOOP, 2026-10-21, after root's note): main (4328d02, CONFIG) merged in (acede70); the source is config, not SEON_SOURCE: `:seon.config.source/root` (absent: no import, no write-back; a run states it with `--set`), `:seon.config.source/dirs`, `:seon.config.source/branch`, read in `seon.root/start!`; every `{:seon.db/edn true}` config key is stored as EDN text by its schema (`seon.config/facts`, `seon.config/from-db`), not a hand list. Two reds were at their cause: the seed (`seon.db/seed-tx`) records `:var/kind :fn` since this branch, so its uncontracted example functions were refused at merge (smoke: a3's doc change on app.m0/f1); the example program now carries contracts, as every function must. And a root's HOME is its checkout's target/home (main's per-checkout HOME), which holds no git identity, so the loop's commit-tree failed 'Author identity unknown'; Seon commits as `:seon.config.source/committer-name` / `committer-email` (`seon.fx/git-commit`). bin/check 100 CHECK OK at target/check/20261021T152137 (2a079e5). `reference-code/reference-code.link` deleted. Merging these four tasks puts merges on disk: a merge writes `next/src` files (`seon.source`, `seon.fx/write-file`) and commits them on `jolt/seon-merges` (`seon.fx/git-commit`)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/needs #{"seon.program/definitions-from-the-compiler"}
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.source-test/importing-src-records-every-form-exactly}}
 {:seon.task/name "merged-forms-write-back-exactly"
  :seon.task/plan "A typed file effect writes main's merged program back to its files: each namespace's file is its ns form and its forms in recorded order, exact text; untouched forms byte-identical. Proof of fidelity: import src, write back with no merge, zero diff (on a copy of the tree, never the live tree). Merged with the first (docs/source.md#write-back-mains-files-byte-for-byte)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/needs #{"seon.source/seon-source-is-the-program"}
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.source-test/import-then-write-back-is-a-zero-diff
                      seon.source-test/a-merged-change-rewrites-only-its-form}}
 {:seon.task/name "merges-are-committed-and-run-next"
  :seon.task/plan "A git commit effect commits written files on branch jolt/seon-merges of next/ (never main, never pushed), its message naming the merged tasks and attempts from facts; the asker records the sha. The next generation: build, root restarted on the same store (resume), the merged fix running. The first full loop: a task changes a seon.* function, merges, is written, committed, rebuilt, and the new root runs it. Seon is the commit's author and committer (`:seon.config.source/committer-name`, `committer-email`): a root's environment has no git identity of its own, and the proof's tree has none either, so the proof fails if the identity is dropped. bin/check loop commits on a clone (LOOP OK, target/check/20261021T152137/e2e-loop.log)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/needs #{"seon.source/merged-forms-write-back-exactly"}
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.source-test/a-merge-is-committed-on-the-merges-branch}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-next-generation-runs-from-the-merges-commit"
  :seon.task/plan "Session LOOP proved the first full turn by hand (2026-10-21): bin/check loop committed ce00b59 on its clone's jolt/seon-merges (seon.fixtures/generation 1 -> 2, one line), the file from that commit built in an APFS copy of the worktree (bin/build seon, 44 s), and that binary's root, resumed on the run's kept store (SEON_STORE), answered (seon.fixtures/generation) = 2 with main still holding the merged attempt and its sha. Automate it as the loop's last stage: a typed :build effect (a worktree of the merges branch with the pinned submodules, bin/build seon under the build lock, the binary's path or the build's error) and root's restart on the same store as an owner act recorded on main (the generation: commit sha, binary, boot). Also: main's source is imported only when main holds none, so commits made to the tree outside Seon (a worktree session's) are not seen by a resumed store; importing the tree's new commit should arrive as a merge into main like any branch (its changes since the imported commit), so agents' merged forms and the owner's edits meet in one three-way. Session LOOP, 2026-10-21, before building it: (1) a resumed root is named by config now, not env: `--set :seon.config.root/store-path=...` and `--set :seon.config.root/main=...` (default.edn still reads SEON_STORE/SEON_MAIN through #env, which bin/check sets); the build effect's binary is started with those and `:seon.config.source/root` pointing at the merges worktree. (2) Restarting root from inside root is a change to who owns the process lifetime (bin/root double-forks and owns it; nothing in src replaces its own process), so it needs root's go on the design before code: either the :build effect only answers a binary and the owner act stays bin/root's (`bin/root restart BINARY` reading the generation fact), or a supervising proc. (3) Resuming a store an earlier build wrote is not safe yet: a root on this worktree's runs/store (written at 14:56 by an intermediate build of this branch) answered `{:op :agents}` with a seon.task/entity-value invalid-output on the :root entity and then never answered a root eval (seon.root/a-root-resumes-a-store-an-earlier-build-wrote); a fresh store answered all. That task needs to merge first. (4) Build cost: bin/build seon 40-48 s warm in this worktree (14:50, 14:53, 15:29 runs), under the one build lock. Owner decision 2026-10-21 (DECISIONS, option a): `bin/root` restarts the next generation, not root itself. The `:build` effect builds the merges commit and records the generation on main as a fact (commit sha, binary path); `bin/root` stops the root and starts it on that binary, resuming the store with `--set` on `:seon.config.root/store-path` and `:seon.config.root/main`. No supervising proc inside root. Session NEXTGEN, 2026-10-21, built as decided (docs/source.md#the-next-generation): after `:seon.attempt/commit` the reconciler asks the `:build` effect (`seon.fx/build`: `bin/worktree --at` makes `<:seon.config.source/generations-dir>/<sha>` a detached worktree of the source repository with this checkout's submodule repos at the commit's pins and its target/ cloned, then `bin/build seon` there under `:seon.config.source/build-lock`, in the tree's own environment) and writes `:seon.root/generation` on main's root entity (`seon.reconcile/generation`, `my.root/generation`); `bin/root next` asks the running root for `my.root/next-generation` (the tree and the `--set` arguments: store-path, main, source root = the generation's tree, dirs, branch, generations-dir, build-lock), stops it and starts the tree's binary (`bin/root run`, the tree's own config, caches and LMDB build). `seon.e2e/loop-turn!` is the turn through the server; `bin/check loop` now also builds and removes the generation's tree. Two things the code held that the plan did not say: `bin/build seon` rebuilt LMDB from ../vendor-c on every run, which a generation's tree under runs/ has no sibling of, so it now builds LMDB when missing as it does Chez and Jolt; and `seon.fx/kinds` was dead (system.edn declares the kinds) and is deleted. Measured: the turn 53 s, the build 43 s of it (worktree ~4 s), `bin/root next` 4.0 s from stop to the new root listening (open 12 ms, preload 156 ms), the new binary answering `(seon.fixtures/generation)` = 2 on the resumed main. Still open from this plan: commits to the tree made outside Seon do not reach a resumed main (a three-way of the tree's new commit into main); old generations' trees are kept with the run until the owner reclaims them."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/needs #{"seon.source/merges-are-committed-and-run-next" "seon.root/a-root-resumes-a-store-an-earlier-build-wrote"}
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.source-test/a-restart-catches-up-and-a-new-generation-resumes}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-tests-are-part-of-the-program"
  :seon.task/plan "Found by root 2026-10-21, starting the first DeepSeek agent on Seon's own code: a task's proofs are deftests in `test/`, so a root must import `test/` with `src/` (`:seon.config.source/dirs [\"src\" \"test\"]`) for an agent's proofs to run on its branch. That root failed at boot: `seon.source/import-tx` -> `seon.source/text-tx` -> `seon.source/accounts` -> `seon.source/analyze-form` threw `No such namespace: d` for a test file's `d/q`. The analyzer resolves a form's aliases against namespaces the process has loaded; the binary never loads Seon's test namespaces, and `seon.source-test`'s helper `require`s every namespace before importing, which hid it. Correct: a form is analyzed against its own file's ns form (its requires and aliases), loaded or not, so any file imports; the root then imports src and test, and a task's proofs resolve on its branch. Then rerun the first agent: `seon.reconcile/a-refused-spawn-is-recorded` on a root with source dirs src and test (root's run config in `~/src/seon-jolt/runs-ds/run1.edn`). Session TESTS, 2026-10-21: the cause was narrower than the analyzer: `seon.source/analyze-form` runs an unloaded namespace's ns form, but read that as `(find-ns ns)` after `seon.program/ensure-ns!` had just created it, so it never ran. Two more gaps behind it, both fixed: the reader read the whole file before the ns form ran (`::config/checkout` in seon.config-test: Invalid token), so `seon.source/read-forms` takes a step run before the next read; and a file requiring a namespace only the import defines at a later path (r3.inc-test -> r3.oracle) failed to locate it, so `seon.source/import-tx` analyzes files in require order (`seon.program/topo` over each ns form's analyzed :requires). Loaded now means `clojure.core/loaded-libs`. Proven: a root booted with dirs src, test/r3, test/next, test/build imports 64 files (2.26 s); in that root the other 26 files of test/seon analyze (228 ms). Not done: `seon.print-test` requires `fipp.engine`, which needs `clojure.core.rrb-vector`, and a built binary cannot load a stdlib namespace it did not compile (its source roots \"stdlib\", \"jolt-core\" are relative to the cwd, target/): task `jolt.ns/a-built-binary-requires-a-stdlib-namespace-it-did-not-compile`. Until then a root with dirs [\"src\" \"test\"] fails at boot on that file."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.source-test/a-file-whose-namespace-is-not-loaded-imports}}
 {:seon.task/name "schemas-are-part-of-the-program"
  :seon.task/plan "Found by root 2026-10-21 preparing the first DeepSeek agent on Seon's own code: the first task (`seon.reconcile/a-refused-spawn-is-recorded`) needs `:refused` added to `:seon.attempt/status`, whose definition is in `resources/seon/schemas/seon.facts.edn`. The program an agent changes is Clojure forms (`seon.source` imports `.clj` files under `:seon.config.source/dirs`), so no agent can change a schema, and most real fixes touch one. v1's rule (CLAUDE.md, 'The program is entities: functions, tests, schemas, namespaces, render pairs, contracts'): schemas are program entities, changed on a branch and merged like a function. Correct: a schema resource (the EDN files under `resources/seon/schemas/`) is imported as program data, an agent changes an entry at its REPL on its branch (a function that writes the entry, like `defn` writes a var), the merge carries it per key (`seon.merge/three-way` over program keys), and write-back rewrites only the changed entries of the file, byte for byte elsewhere (as `seon.source/file-text` does for forms). The schema registry the running generation uses comes from the build as today; the next generation builds the merged file. The owner's model (DECISIONS 2026-10-21): \"EDN for offline compiled into program and register at the repl and that can write to the edn if it's merged to main.\" Port v1's mechanism, read first and not redesigned (owner, 2026-10-21: \"read how we did it in seon and don't redo work\"): an agent changes a schema by calling `register!` at its REPL (v1 `~/src/seon/src/seon/schema.clj` `seon.schema/register!`: one declaration `{:seon.schema/key k :seon.schema/definition v}`, validated against the whole population, a refusal returned as data); the registration is a schema entity on the agent's branch (`:seon.schema/key`, `:seon.schema/form`, a definition digest; v1 `seon.program/schema-entities`), a program identity `[:seon.schema/key k]` that merges per key like a var; write-back rewrites only the changed entries of the resource file with rewrite-clj, every other node keeping its text (v1 `~/src/seon/src/seon/cluster/source.clj` `schema-file-text`, `schema-entry-edit`), detects an entry the file holds in neither side's form as a conflict, and is proven by reading the candidate back (v1 `proven-schema-file`). Map these onto next's owners (`seon.source` for import and write-back, `seon.merge` for the per-key merge, `seon.contract`'s registry for the running generation, `docs/source.md`), dropping v1's machinery next does not have (candidate collectors, projections, SCI). Plan before code: the plan names each v1 function ported and what it becomes, and goes to the owner before code."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.source-test/a-schema-file-round-trips-through-the-program}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-merge-writes-back-only-what-it-changed"
  :seon.task/plan "Found by worktree session store-native, 2026-10-22, in bin/check 100's e2e-loop on a stale binary (target/seon built from main e117b9b; the source tree at jolt/store-native, which adds the private fn `seon.db/stored-id`): the loop's merge commit (loop/gen-merge#1, target/check/20261022T182512/loop/tree commit f3ad2ad) changed src/seon/fixtures.clj as the task asked and also deleted `stored-id` from src/seon/db.clj (12 lines), so LOOP FAILED. On a binary rebuilt from the same source the loop passes (LOOP OK, :files src/seon/fixtures.clj only). So a form present in the source root imports but absent from the running binary is lost by the merge's write-back: the merge's delta, or the write-back's rendering of db.clj, treats it as retracted. A binary older than its source is the normal case after a merge loads in place (bin/root next: only a pin, deps.edn or native code needs a new binary), so this can delete merged code. Cause not found (not investigated: outside the session's task). Correct: the write-back renders exactly the files whose forms the merge changed, from main's live forms, whatever the binary holds; proof: the loop on a binary that lacks one source form commits only the task's file."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.e2e-test/a-merge-on-an-older-binary-keeps-the-forms-it-lacks}}
 {:seon.task/name "an-import-reads-a-changed-ns-form-before-its-forms"
  :seon.task/plan "Seen by session DUP-HELPERS, 2026-10-22: a root started on a binary older than its source refuses to start when one source file's ns form gained an alias that a later form of the same file uses. Reproduction: the binary built before src/my/schema.clj's ns form added [seon.config :as config] and a form cited config/schemas-dir; `./seon root` on that tree (as seon.agent-test/in-agent runs it) died in seon.root/import-source! -> seon.source/replace-tx -> text-tx -> accounts -> read-forms -> analyze-form with `No such namespace: config`, so every in-agent test and seon.source-test/a-restart-catches-up-and-a-new-generation-resumes errored until the binary was rebuilt. A binary older than its source is the normal case after a merge (task seon.source/a-merge-writes-back-only-what-it-changed), so a merged require must not stop the next boot. Cause, to verify: the import analyzes each form against the namespace the binary holds, whose aliases are the old ns form's; the changed ns form is not applied (or its requires not read) before the forms after it. Correct: the file's ns form is analyzed and its requires and aliases applied first, then its forms. Proof: a source file whose ns form adds a require of a loaded namespace and whose later defn cites it through the new alias imports on a root that held the old ns form. Done 2026-10-22 (worktree session DUP-HELPERS), after the same cause stopped the owner's standing root at main 6848851 (`No such namespace: ts`): at boot seon.program/load-all! runs main's program, including test namespaces' old ns forms, and an old ns form's require of another test namespace (seon.program-test, r3.fx) marks that one loaded, so seon.source/analyze-form skipped its new ns form (it ran ns forms only for namespaces not in loaded-libs) and the forms citing ts/ did not analyze. Now analyze-form always runs a file's ns form first, as loading the file would; running it again on the namespace it set up changes nothing. The gap that kept checks green: the standing root imports test/ (the main tree's config/local.edn) and the default did not; :seon.config.source/dirs now defaults to src, test and the schemas, so every root a check or test starts with a source root imports what the owner's does."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.source-test/a-changed-ns-form-applies-its-requires-before-its-forms}}
 {:seon.task/name "test-namespaces-built-into-the-binary"
  :seon.task/plan "Measured 2026-10-22 (worktree session DUP-HELPERS, bin/root start on the M1, nice 15, main 9382656): a root that imports test/ beside src and the schemas boots in 8.7-9.1 s on restart against 3.5-3.6 s without it, and holds 119-130 MB against 111 MB (seon.lib/real-mb; 382 namespaces against 333); fresh, 8.7 s and 199 MB against 5.8 s and 180 MB, the import itself 4.5 s for 131 files and 1,923 forms against 2.9 s for 80 and 1,288. The ~5 s a restart is seon.program/load-all! evaluating every test form one by one at each boot, since the binary (bin/build seon: jolt build -m seon.check) compiles src only and never holds them. The owner's standing root imports test/ (the main tree's config/local.edn) and pays it on every restart; the default dirs leave test/ out for this reason. Correct: the binary holds the test namespaces as it holds src's (built with them, so load-all! finds them held and loads none), and a root importing test/ boots in about the src-only time. Then :seon.config.source/dirs can default to the standing root's dirs. Check first what v1 did and whether Jolt's build can take a second source root (jolt build's :paths / deps alias :test) without the test runner's main. Proof: a restart of a root importing src and test/ costs no more than 1.2x a src-only restart (a cost proof over the boot, measured with seon.profile/now-ns)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.source-test/a-root-importing-test-restarts-as-fast-as-src}}]
